The bug is that `appendQuotedString(..., escapingOn=true)` can return without advancing `pos` when the current character is a quote. Its callers loop on `pos`, so leaving `pos` unchanged causes an infinite loop and unbounded appends, which matches the observed `OutOfMemoryError`.

The patch fixes exactly that:

```java
if (escapingOn && c[start] == QUOTE) {
    next(pos);
    return appendTo == null ? null : appendTo.append(QUOTE);
}
```

Now every time that early-return path is taken, the parse position advances by one, so the caller cannot get stuck on the same quote forever.

This is not just suppressing the specific test case. The root cause was “early return without consuming input,” and the patch removes that root cause for that branch entirely. I do not see another concrete input that would still reach the same infinite-loop/OOM via this path after the change.

VERDICT: CORRECT

